WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.913
In this video, you're
going to see the future.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.330
Not everyone is trying

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.290
to win a popularity contest.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.833
Case in point, this guy.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.160
I don't think that popularity

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.700
is something he's interested in.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.520
He's interested in effectiveness.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.570
I've gotta give it up.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.590
His air time, so to speak, is only a tool

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.480
to achieve what his
greatest aspiration is,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.640
which is to have a positive
impact on the world.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.800
You should look at, you
know, what was your goal,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.430
how are you trying to uplift nutrition

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:31.380
and survival and literacy.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.980
He is influential, A,
because of his vast fortune

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.940
and the way he made it,
by creating a new segment

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.510
of the computer market.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.450
But also, that he's since then,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.710
chosen to deploy the money he's made

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.410
to try to completely alter
the landscape of world health.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.120
This year his net worth reached

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.313
as high as $120 billion.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.900
These two have donated more money

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.070
to charitable causes than anyone ever.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.430
He's a multidimensional individual.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.860
He has great depth in
so many different areas,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.083
I guess you'd call him a polymath.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.790
William Henry Gates the third

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.910
was born in Seattle Washington in 1955.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.348
He was a terrible child,

00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:19.100
Bill senior gave a whole interview

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.830
to the Wall Street Journal
about just how difficult he was,

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.720
how much trouble he gave his mother.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.910
You know, yelling, screaming,
tantrums, things like that.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.580
At the ripe age of 13,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.540
the problem child wrote
his first software program.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:37.460
A Tic Tac Toe game on the
Teletype Model 33ASR teleprinter.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.970
Eight years later, he
dropped out of Harvard

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.750
to start a computer software company

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.390
with his best friend Paul
Allen, called Microsoft.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.340
In co-founding Microsoft, what Bill Gates

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.110
and Paul Allen did was,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.760
essentially create the personal
computer software market.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.090
That's since then been worth

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.863
hundreds of billions of dollars.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.910
This is Jeff Raikes,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.430
he's known Bill for quite some time now.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:05.380
I was at Microsoft from 1981 to 2008

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.230
and Bill was completely
committed to the vision

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.330
that we could help put
a computer on every desk

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.510
and in every home, running great software.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.940
And that's the impact
that he wanted to have

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.393
and he was completely focused in on that.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:22.730
Now, such determination

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.420
does require a strong
personality, and it's safe to say

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.610
that his subordinates
could probably relate

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.150
to his parents at that point.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.020
Well, he was a very tough
manager, people were,

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.160
had these sort of terrifying
Bill Gates reviews

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.220
of their products and features.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.040
And if he didn't like what
you said he would, you know,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.090
he was known for
responding with the words.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.390
Well that's the stupidest
thing I've ever heard.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.113
But the good news is, you
won't hold the record for long.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.350
Seattle's Bill Gates, with $4.2 billion,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.150
is the second richest
person in the country.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.790
What is this?

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.760
It's Microsoft Excel, now hold still,

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.380
I'm about to perform a miracle.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.173
My spreadsheet doesn't do that.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.320
Microsoft became such a juggernaut

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.200
that the following decade they were sued

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.850
for being a monopoly.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.850
If you look at that
point in the year 2000,

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.585
and Microsoft is in
serious anti-trust trouble

00:03:17.585 --> 00:03:20.410
and the view of Bill at
that time was terrible.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.320
And people viewed him

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.983
as a sharp elbowed,
monopolist essentially.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:27.553
Not that he cared

00:03:27.553 --> 00:03:29.040
about what the world thought of him,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.030
but it was around this
time that Bill decided

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.400
to start listening to his parents.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.970
I do remember in the
early days of Microsoft,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.470
his mother pressing young
Bill to get involved

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.500
with the united way and philanthropy

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.410
and Bill's response was you
know, Mom, I'm you know,

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.800
I'm trying to build this company,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.850
I don't really have time for that.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.500
And I think over time, what he learned is,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.330
in the same way that he could leverage

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.530
his intellectual energy
on behalf of Microsoft,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.980
he could do it on behalf of philanthropy.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.930
While in the midst of
this anti-trust suit,

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:05.480
Bill and his wife Melinda donated
$16 billion worth of stock

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.670
to charity in 1999.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.320
The following year he
poured $5.1 billion in cash

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.530
to create the Bill and
Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.410
At this point I think people, many people,

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.590
much more closely
associate him with programs

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.790
that have saved lives and you know,

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:25.140
increased immunizations
in the developing world.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.710
That's much more the popular
view of Bill at this point,

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.110
that sort of philanthropist

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.760
and not the monopolist so to speak.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.830
So you can avoid lots of diseases

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.450
if you've been properly vaccinated.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:39.700
Today, the Foundation

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.550
is the world's largest charity.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.660
It's fingerprint is on almost
every global health initiative

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.930
of the last 20 years.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.250
And has spent over $40
billion on global development

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.770
and public health programs.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.260
Yes, they deploy financial resources

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.690
but they also use their voice.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:59.170
The networks of influence
and support that they have.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.590
So in many ways, it represents
one of the key concepts

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.420
that we learned on software,
it's about leverage.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:09.530
What Bill wants, what Melinda
wants, with the Foundation

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.950
is to be able to make
those financial investments

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.360
and leverage them to have
the greatest possible impact.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.440
By using this approach to philanthropy,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.160
the Foundation provided seed
money to The Global Fund

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.830
to fight aids, tuberculosis and malaria.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.900
Their implementation of immunizations

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.080
in the developing world has
contributed to a dramatic drop

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:32.770
in the overall child
mortality rate since 1990.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.670
Bill also made it his personal mission

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:36.880
to eradicate polio from the planet,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.820
something he has come
very close to achieving.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:43.090
Then of course there's Bill's
noblest pursuit of all.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.780
I wanna talk to you today about toilets.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.810
Many people would say, well
why get involved in toilets

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:51.730
and how does that matter.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:54.150
But that, you know, actually
kinda diving into that

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.580
explains a bit about how
Bill thinks about things.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.320
If you want to reduce the disease burden,

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.200
you're gonna have to improve sanitation.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.640
If you're gonna improve sanitation,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.040
you're gonna have to invent
a new way to go about it,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.000
one that doesn't require all
of the water that we use today.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.140
So it's a great example of how he thinks

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.210
in terms of the broader system

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.560
and looks for technological innovations

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.933
that can really make a difference.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.210
This push to improve sanitation worldwide

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.720
has led to the invention
of the Omniprocessor,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.030
a low cost human waste treatment plant

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.120
with a beta version currently
in use in Dakar, Senegal.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.890
I am very impressed

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.500
with this solution we're seeing here.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.840
And it generates electricity,
it generates clean water.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.510
In the area of climate change,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.280
Gates has embraced the
often unpopular solution

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.600
of nuclear energy as an
alternative to fossil fuels.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.320
The energy has to come from somewhere.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.910
You're giving up all coal,
all natural gas, all oil.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.580
It is a good example of both, in the fact

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.850
that he isn't concerned about
public opinion or popularity,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.320
but grounded in what he believes

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:04.420
to be the data and the facts.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.200
A Gates company called Terrapower

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.610
was very close to building the
world's first nuclear reactor

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.820
fueled by actual nuclear waste, in China.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.910
But progress was stopped in its tracks

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.393
when Trump launched a trade
war against China in 2018.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.500
No sooner was this vision put on hold,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.090
than did Gates have a more urgent calling.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.590
This is going to be era defining.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.250
And no one was in a better place

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.010
to say, I told you so.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:35.290
The most urgent invention
in the world right now

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:39.570
is a vaccine that prevents
you from getting COVID-19.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.840
The fact that he has such depth

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.540
across so many areas makes him unique

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.950
at being able to take
on the current pandemic.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.700
He has now spent 20 years
learning about funding programs,

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.060
talking to leading researchers
on things like viruses,

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.270
things like vaccines.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.510
Some of the programs in the Seattle area,

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.030
including the Seattle flu study

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.550
that caught some of the
first COVID cases here,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.080
are things that have been
funded out of the Foundation.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:08.720
So far, Gates has chipped in

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.310
a total of $300 million and counting

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.150
in the fight against coronavirus.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.450
They're focused on detection, isolation

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:19.183
and treatment of the virus
and finding a vaccine.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.480
This chapter in Bill's life

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:23.730
has yet to be written,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.570
and could have the highest
stakes he's had to face yet.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.630
His intellectual intensity

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:32.820
sometimes carries over
to big expectations.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.300
But at the end of the day,

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.090
the person he's toughest on, is himself.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.120
If he doesn't figure out something

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.130
that he thinks he should have figured out,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.650
he'll be disappointed in himself.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.680
It drives him, it motivates him,

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:48.620
it gives him a sense of urgency
to really make a difference.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.373
And I think that's one of
his greatest attributes.

